{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Ring.Finset"],"declarations":12,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.conventionD_z_solves_bridge_at_r_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.conventionD_z_solves_bridge_at_r_two","kind":"theorem","line":192,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MarkovNormalisation.lean#L192-L206","doc":"**Convention D is the `r = 2` bridge solution.**\n\nWith `Q = q²`, the `r = 2` bridge `q·z = Q²/(1+Q)` holds for\n`z = q³/(q²+1)`.\n\nSo `z = q³/(q²+1)` is not a convention *chosen* over `1/(q^{1/2}+q^{-1/2})` —\nit is the value solving the bridge at an integer `r = 2`, and `r` is the\nnumber of variables in the Schur principal specialisation. Convention C\nsolves the same equation at a non-integer `r`, which is why nothing vanishes\nthere (`wenzl_factor_nonzero_of_ne`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.bridge_rhs_at_r_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.bridge_rhs_at_r_two","kind":"theorem","line":184,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MarkovNormalisation.lean#L184-L190","doc":"The bridge right-hand side at `r = 2` collapses: the `(1-Q)` factor\ncancels against `1-Q² = (1-Q)(1+Q)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.wenzl_factor_nonzero_of_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.wenzl_factor_nonzero_of_ne","kind":"theorem","line":158,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MarkovNormalisation.lean#L158-L170","doc":"Convention C does **not** vanish there. With `z = 1/(q^{1/2} + q^{-1/2})`\nthe bridge solves to a non-integer `r`, so `X ≠ Q²` and the `(2,0)` factor\n`1 - X·Q^{-2}` is nonzero — which is why every convention-C weight on a\nthree-row partition is live. Stated as the contrapositive so it is\nfalsifiable rather than definitional. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.wenzl_prod_vanishes_of_content_neg_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.wenzl_prod_vanishes_of_content_neg_two","kind":"theorem","line":139,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MarkovNormalisation.lean#L139-L156","doc":"**The vanishing, for any partition shape.**\n\nThe Wenzl-bridge weight of `λ` carries `∏_{x ∈ λ} (1 - X · Q^{c(x)})` over the\ncells of `λ`. Under convention D, `X = Q²`. This says: if *any* cell has\ncontent `-2`, the whole product is zero — so the conclusion does not depend on\nthe shape of `λ` beyond it having such a cell.\n\nEvery partition with three or more rows has one, at `(i,j) = (2,0)` where\n`c = j - i = -2`. Cells are left abstract (`s : Finset ι` with contents\n`c : ι → ℤ`) precisely so the lemma is about the *content*, not about a\nparticular encoding of Young diagrams. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.wenzl_factor_vanishes_on_third_row","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.wenzl_factor_vanishes_on_third_row","kind":"theorem","line":135,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MarkovNormalisation.lean#L135-L137","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.markov_normalisation_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.markov_normalisation_two","kind":"theorem","line":93,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MarkovNormalisation.lean#L93-L105","doc":"**Markov normalisation at `n = 2`, convention D.**\n\n`∑_{λ ⊢ 2} y_λ · dim V_λ = 1`, both dimensions being `1`.\n\nNote both weights are *live* here: `n = 2` admits no partition with three\nrows, so the vanishing that characterises convention D has nothing to act on.\n`n = 3` is the smallest case where D and C differ, which is why that is the\ninteresting instance. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.yTwoSign","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.yTwoSign","kind":"def","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MarkovNormalisation.lean#L90-L91","doc":"The convention-D weight on `(1,1) ⊢ 2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.yTwoTriv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.yTwoTriv","kind":"def","line":87,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MarkovNormalisation.lean#L87-L88","doc":"The convention-D weight on `(2) ⊢ 2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.markov_normalisation_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.markov_normalisation_three","kind":"theorem","line":74,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MarkovNormalisation.lean#L74-L85","doc":"**Markov normalisation at `n = 3`, convention D.**\n\n`∑_{λ ⊢ 3} y_λ · dim V_λ = 1`, with `dim V_{(3)} = 1`, `dim V_{(2,1)} = 2`,\n`dim V_{(1,1,1)} = 1`.\n\nAn identity of rational functions in `q`, hypothesised only on\n`q^2 + 1 ≠ 0` — no substrate value is involved. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.ySign","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.ySign","kind":"def","line":66,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MarkovNormalisation.lean#L66-L72","doc":"The convention-D Jones–Markov weight on the sign partition `(1,1,1) ⊢ 3`.\n\nZero because `r = 2`: a Schur polynomial in two variables vanishes on a\npartition with three rows. This is the structural content of convention D,\nand the property that distinguishes it from convention C, where the\ncorresponding weight is nonzero. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.yStd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.yStd","kind":"def","line":62,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MarkovNormalisation.lean#L62-L64","doc":"The convention-D Jones–Markov weight on the standard partition\n`(2,1) ⊢ 3`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.yTriv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.MarkovNormalisation.yTriv","kind":"def","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/MarkovNormalisation.lean#L59-L60","doc":"The convention-D Jones–Markov weight on the trivial partition `(3) ⊢ 3`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}